 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$, where $N$ is the number of voxels, as each voxel requires a constant number of lookups and arithmetic operations [S5].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing the gradient field separately [S5].
-*   **Performance:** Very fast due to minimal stencil size (6 neighbors) compared to Sobel (26 neighbors) or Neumann (26+ neighbors) [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Low computational cost, simple implementation, suitable for real-time rendering when precomputed gradients are not feasible or when on-the-fly estimation is required [S1, S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Less smooth gradients compared to operators using more voxels (e.g., Sobel3D, Neumann), resulting in a worse description of local surface structure [S2]. Visual artifacts similar to trilinear interpolation resampling can occur [S3, S14].
-*   **When to use:** Real-time volume rendering where performance is critical, or when implementing on-the-fly shading without precomputed gradient textures.
-*   **When not to use:** High-fidelity offline rendering where smooth shading is required, or when noise reduction is needed (use Sobel or Neumann instead).
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels, as each voxel requires a constant number of reads and arithmetic operations [S5].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing the gradient field if precomputed [S5]. If computed on-the-fly during raycasting, memory overhead is minimal but bandwidth usage increases due to 6 extra texture fetches per sample [S2].
+*   **Performance:** The central difference operator is significantly faster than Sobel or Zucker-Hummel operators because it requires only 6 sampling points instead of 54 for the Zucker-Hummel operator [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, low computational cost, provides reasonable shading normals for smooth volumes.
+*   **Limitations:** Less smooth than Sobel operators; sensitive to noise in the scalar field; provides a worse description of local surface structure compared to larger kernels [S2]. Boundary handling via padding or clamping can introduce visual artifacts similar to trilinear interpolation resampling [S3, S14].
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from standard numerical analysis techniques for approximating derivatives. In volume rendering, it was popularized for real-time raycasting to enable local illumination effects like Phong shading without the cost of explicit surface extraction [S1, S2]. It is widely used in GPU-based volume renderers, often implemented as compute shaders or fragment shaders for on-the-fly gradient estimation [S4, S8, S10]. The technique is documented in standard graphics references such as the NVIDIA GPU Gems [S3] and various academic dissertations on real-time volume rendering [S6].
+In volume rendering, it was popularized for real-time raycasting to enable Phong shading and opacity modulation without precomputing complex derivative fields [S1, S2]. It is widely used in systems like VolumePro, which implemented hardware-accelerated gradient estimation using FIFO buffers to pipeline the computation [S12]. It is also a standard component in scientific visualization libraries like CGAL for isosurfacing [S15].
 
